# TCB production-path panic-surface ratchet. Read by
# scripts/check_tcb_panic_freedom.sh; the gate FAILS if the live count exceeds
# the number below. Ratchet DOWN only. Whoever moves it records why here.
#
# 2026-07-24 — 180 -> 297. Two independent things were wrong at once.
#
#   Measurement. The 180 was produced by a raw line grep that counted comment
#   prose and string-literal text as panic sites. In the TCB that is not a
#   rounding error: vcgen and trust_verify DOCUMENT the panicking code they
#   reason about, so 107 of the 404 the old counter reported at this commit
#   were sentences, not statements — and the old number could be lowered by
#   deleting explanations. The counter now erases comments and literal
#   contents and drops every `#[cfg(test)]` item whole before matching. Its
#   answer at the 2026-06-23 snapshot commit (8a653fe5cb) is 167, which lands
#   on the hand-classified "real code" table in reports/tcb-hardening-audit.md
#   (unwrap 15 = 15, unreachable 47 vs 48); the 180 never matched even its own
#   counter, which read 201 on that tree.
#
#   Drift. Measured consistently, the code surface still grew 167 -> 297 over
#   the ~3700 commits since the snapshot, concentrated in trust-types (+33),
#   trust-cg-bridge (+26), trust-vcgen (+20), trust_verify (+16). The gate was
#   red that whole time, so it caught none of it. 297 is the honest count
#   today, not an acceptable one: it is a burn-down backlog, and the ratchet
#   works again from here. Reproduce it from a clean checkout at d9616037e0:
#   scripts/check_tcb_panic_freedom.sh --print.
# 2026-07-25: 297 -> 283. `chc::binop_to_formula` deleted — it was a thin
# panicking wrapper (`unwrap_or_else(|err| panic!(...))`) over the fallible
# `try_binop_to_formula`, sitting in the verification path. It had NO production
# callers left: trust-transval had already migrated with a comment saying
# "rather than panicking inside chc::binop_to_formula -- the verifier core", and
# the two remaining in-crate sites now pass the Result straight through their
# match arms. Test call sites unwrap, which is where unwrapping belongs.
# 2026-08-26: 283 -> 281. TWO separate things happened; read both, because the
#   number moved DOWN while the real panic surface had gone UP.
#
#   1. THE COUNTER WAS WRONG, and it over-counted. The cfg(test) stripper cleared
#      its `pend` flag after exactly ONE line, so it only stripped a
#      `#[cfg(test)]` item whose brace sat on the very next line. The ubiquitous
#          #[cfg(test)]
#          #[test]
#          fn t() { ... a.unwrap() ... }
#      form therefore leaked its ENTIRE BODY into the production count: `#[test]`
#      carries no brace, `pend` reset, and `fn t() {` was read as production
#      code. That contradicted the gate's own docstring ("every `#[cfg(test)]`
#      item -- `mod`, `fn`, `impl`, anything -- is dropped whole"). It now scans
#      forward to the item, which is bounded because an item is either braced or
#      `;`-terminated. Mutation-proved on four shapes: attributed fn, plain
#      `mod`, multi-line attribute, and `mod tests;`.
#
#   2. THERE WAS ALSO A REAL REGRESSION, and the counter bug was hiding its size
#      rather than causing it. Measured with the CORRECTED lexer on both trees:
#      the baseline commit 823eafeeff measures 281, HEAD measured 303 -- a
#      genuine +22 of production panic surface, not +27 and not 0. Per-root:
#      trust_verify.rs +6, trust-vcgen +6, trust-ir-bridge +5, trust-types +3,
#      trust-cg-bridge +2.
#
#   The +22 was PAID DOWN rather than absorbed, since this ratchet only ever goes
#   down. Every replacement is TOTAL, not relocated -- no counted panic was
#   swapped for an uncounted one (an index, a bare `+`, a `saturating_*` that
#   loops), which would game the metric instead of reducing risk:
#     * `_ => unreachable!()` arms under an outer `32 | 64` / `Add | Sub` binding
#       -> two-way `if`/`else`, total by the outer pattern (float_overflow.rs x3,
#       lower.rs x2).
#     * `len() == 1` followed by "therefore Some" -> let `pop` decide the shape
#       (guards.rs x3, lower.rs x1). Semantics identical, no assertion step.
#     * solver `try_not`/`try_assert_term` `.expect()` -> fail CLOSED to
#       `Discharge::Unknown`, matching `Solver::try_new` two lines above
#       (verify_output.rs x2).
#     * `char::from_digit(nibble, 16).expect("nibble < 16")` -> a 16-element
#       table; both nibbles of a u8 are statically in 0..=15 (model.rs x4).
#     * `checked_add(1).expect("a finite MIR body must have an unoccupied block
#        identity")` -> `smallest_free_block_id`, which STATES the pigeonhole
#       argument the expect stood in for (model.rs x2).
#     * `let ... else { unreachable!() }` where an arm guard already established
#       the shape -> take the documented fail-visible fallback (`TrustIrTy::Ptr`,
#       `return None`, `continue`) instead of aborting mid-proof.
#   281 is therefore the honest count AND, by coincidence worth noting, exactly
#   what the previous baseline's own tree measures once counted correctly.
#
#   STILL OUTSTANDING, deliberately not counted as fixed: the 8 added sites in
#   compiler/rustc_mir_transform/src/trust_verify.rs. Validating a change there
#   needs an `x.py` build rather than a cargo check, so they are a separate
#   piece of work; the ratchet is green without them because the crates-side
#   paydown covered the whole regression.
# 2026-08-26 (later): 281 -> 272. The trust_verify.rs work named above as
#   outstanding is now DONE — that file goes 24 -> 15. Nine sites, each taking a
#   fail-closed path the enclosing function ALREADY had, so no new error
#   vocabulary was invented:
#     * `best.next().expect(..)` and the `Unmonitored` evidence arm -> `Err(..)`
#       in two `Result<_, String>` functions that already return `Err` a few
#       lines above.
#     * `evidence.expect("PublishContractRow is only reachable with evidence")`
#       -> `return None` in an `Option`-returning function whose sibling arm is
#       already `None`.
#     * the `TailCall` `let ... else { unreachable!() }` -> `continue`; the loop
#       is iterating a list FILTERED on exactly that shape.
#     * `dfs_stack.last_mut().expect("nonempty DFS stack")` -> match with
#       `None => break`, in a helper whose own comment already promised to stay
#       "fail-closed and total for malformed test/future callers".
#     * four `_ => unreachable!()` arms whose outer arm had already restricted
#       the scrutinee (five arithmetic Formula variants, five comparison
#       variants, the three USize carriers, the three ISize carriers) -> `Err`.
#   Validated with `./x.py check --stage 1`: 13m04s, exit 0, zero errors, and
#   the log shows `Checking rustc_mir_transform` so the crate really was
#   compiled. Then MUTATION-PROVED that this validation can fail at all, because
#   twice this session a gate passed without looking: a deliberate type error
#   planted in this same file takes the scoped
#   `./x.py check --stage 1 compiler/rustc_mir_transform` to rc=1 with 2 errors,
#   and the restored file back to rc=0.
# 2026-09-21: 272 -> 240. A COUNTER CORRECTION, NOT A RATCHET, and the number
#   moved DOWN on BOTH trees by the same 32, so no delta this file ever quoted
#   changes.
#
#   THE COUNTER WAS WRONG, and it over-counted — the third time, and the same
#   shape as 2026-07-24 (comment prose) and 2026-08-26 (leaked test bodies).
#   `.expect(` is not always `Option::expect`. `trust-types/src/spec_parse.rs`
#   and `spec_attrs.rs` each define
#       fn expect(&mut self, expected: &Token, label: &str) -> Result<(), SpecParseError>
#   and call it 32 times between them, EVERY ONE `?`-propagated. An inherent
#   method shadows the std ones and neither `Option` nor `Result` can be `Self`
#   here, so not one of those 32 can panic. They were counted anyway. The number
#   could therefore have been "reduced" by making a spec parser worse, which is
#   the property that makes a metric worthless.
#
#   MEASURED BOTH TREES WITH THE CORRECTED COUNTER, which is the only way to
#   read this: the baseline commit 84cfbacef69 measures 240 (not the 272 it
#   recorded), and the tree at the time the ratchet went red measures 288 (not
#   320). The over-count is a constant 32 across both, so the +48 regression
#   reported on 2026-09-20 was RIGHT and is unaffected. 240 is that same
#   baseline re-expressed in the corrected unit.
#
#   THE GATE STAYS RED, which is the point. Live is 250 against this 240: the
#   +48 has been paid down to +10, not measured away. Paydown so far:
#     native_shadow_request.rs   -8   (typed refusals the fn already had)
#     verify_output.rs          -30   (Formula->ay translation fails closed)
#   The remaining +10 is real and is still owed.
#
#   The correction is receiver-scoped (`self.expect(`) and mutation-proved in
#   four directions in the script's own comment; a genuine `Option::expect` in
#   one of those parser files is still counted.
#
# 2026-09-30 — 240 -> 239. trust_verify.rs's `unreachable!` in the verify
#   pass's artifact-cache read is removed by type: the cache read is generic
#   over its reader, and the pass takes the full artifact or a counted miss.
#   See reports/tcb-hardening-audit.md, "Resolved: trust_verify.rs:4651".
# 2026-10-10 — 239 -> 222. A COUNTER CORRECTION, then a PAYDOWN. They are
#   separate, and the correction is measured on both trees, as on 2026-09-21.
#
#   THE COUNTER WAS WRONG, and it over-counted — the fourth time. A module FILE
#   declared only under `#[cfg(test)]` was read as production: the stripper
#   dropped the `#[cfg(test)] mod x;` LINE, but the file it names was walked
#   like any other unless its NAME looked like a test. The counter now records
#   every `mod` declaration in the TCB (inline-module nesting and `#[path]`
#   resolved, as rustc resolves them) and drops a file only when EVERY
#   declaration of it is test-only; one production declaration, or none found,
#   keeps it counted. On main every non-root TCB file resolves to at least one
#   declaration and no declaration resolves to a missing file. `--print` names
#   each dropped file and what it would have added. Mutation-proved, in the four
#   directions and more, by scripts/tests/check_tcb_panic_freedom_test.sh.
#
#   MEASURED BOTH TREES WITH THE CORRECTED COUNTER: the baseline commit
#   ba8a0f9fe92 measures 237, not 239 (trust-types patterns.rs x1 and
#   trust-vcgen generate/version_ssa_families.rs x1 are test-only), and main
#   ce026e1c63b measures 236, not 251 (those two, plus trust-cg-bridge
#   compiler_consumer_census.rs x9 and trust-vcgen referent_fold/fold_cost_fix.rs
#   x4). 237 is the old baseline in the corrected unit, and main was inside it
#   by 1 once counted honestly: the 13 production sites it added were offset by
#   14 removed (solver_cache.rs x7, panic_calls.rs x4, overflow_vc.rs x2,
#   vc_artifact_cache.rs x1). The red was the 13 test constructs.
#
#   THE PAYDOWN is those 13 added sites anyway, plus the older twin of one, each
#   now total code or a refusal its function already returns — nothing hidden
#   from the counter, no check deleted:
#     trust-cg-bridge call_event.rs       x5  (frozen; waiver row, fifth movement)
#     trust-cg-bridge compiled_object.rs  x3  (a missing section is `malformed`;
#                                             the local-extent lookup is done once)
#     trust-router ay_discovery.rs        x1  (require_test_ay -> detect_test_ay:
#                                             the NOT RUN panic moved into the tests)
#     trust-types e6_expr/tree.rs         x1  (spine_prefix builds the chain)
#     trust-types formula_hash.rs         x1  (canonical_query_hash returns Result;
#                                             moved here from solver_cache.rs)
#     trust-vcgen loop_bounds.rs          x2  (the DFS edits its top in place; :55
#                                             predates the baseline, :1509 is new)
#     trust-vcgen type_invariant/houdini.rs x1 (indexed iteration)
#   236 - 14 = 222. Per root, old counter -> corrected -> paid down:
#   trust-vcgen 59 -> 54 -> 51, trust-router 24 -> 24 -> 23, trust-types
#   29 -> 28 -> 26, trust-cg-bridge 58 -> 49 -> 41; the other roots unchanged.
#   Reproduce: scripts/check_tcb_panic_freedom.sh --print (and --list).
#
# 2026-10-10 (T4.1 review, branch t4/t41-in-process-liskov-20261010): 222 -> 221.
#   trust-cache fingerprint.rs x1: `hex_digest`'s `.expect` went with the
#   solver-binary fingerprint it served, deleted because no caller reads it
#   since T4.1. Nothing else moved.
#
# 2026-10-15: 221 -> 220. Main (e559e926105) measured 224: the WS7.14 series
#   added four production sites to trust-cg-bridge verify_output_trust_ir.rs,
#   and trust-ir-bridge lower.rs lost one (`stack.last_mut().unwrap()`), net +3.
#   The four are paid down, each by code with no panic path and the same result:
#     * `norm`'s three `if const_pair(..).is_some()` arms (BvOr/BvXor/BvAdd)
#       followed by `const_pair(..).expect("guarded")` -> unguarded arms calling
#       `fold_const_pair`, which folds two constants and otherwise rebuilds the
#       variant unchanged, exactly what the fall-through `other => other` gave.
#     * `find(..).expect("just located")` on a header's located cut -> the
#       function's own `Err`, the same refusal its segment lookup below returns.
#   224 - 4 = 220. Reproduce: scripts/check_tcb_panic_freedom.sh --list.
#
# 2026-10-15 (T3 L4 C06, branch t3/l4-s1-20261011): 220 -> 218.
#   trust-types formula_arena/transform.rs x2 (`unreachable!`): the arena, with no
#   production user, is deleted, and vc_context, which replaces it, adds none. The
#   baseline falls by exactly those two, so they cannot be spent again.
#
# 2026-10-16 (T11, branch inc6/tb1-t11-20261016): 218 -> 206.
#   trust-cache x12, every one in a module T11 deleted as dead (no compiler or
#   verifier path consumed it): alpha_normalize.rs x3, independence.rs x2
#   (`unreachable!`), integrity.rs x2, mir_hash.rs x2, query_cache.rs x1,
#   sub_query_splitter.rs x2. The two modules left, fingerprint.rs and
#   coordination.rs, count 0. Nothing else moved: the base (3a1cb55f071)
#   measured 218. Reproduce: scripts/check_tcb_panic_freedom.sh --list.
#
# 2026-11-04: 206 -> 205. No code changed for this entry; it records a net
#   movement already on main so the one freed site cannot be spent silently.
#   Measured with the SAME (current) counter on both trees: the baseline
#   commit 21bae64e0e9 counts 206, main f2204477fd4 counts 205 (the one
#   intervening counter change, 4b2517912543, only refuses counts from
#   incomplete checkouts and moves no number). Per-site diff of `--list`,
#   line numbers normalized out: four trust-router coordinator.rs `.expect`
#   sites (path.parent, staged.path clone, listener.take, child.stderr.take)
#   are gone with the router rework; three sites were added elsewhere
#   (trust-router full_verification/engine/mod.rs x2 `.expect`, trust-vcgen
#   generate/inferred_return/calls.rs x1 `unreachable!`). -4 +3 = -1. Those
#   three added sites are part of the recorded surface, not excused by this
#   entry; paying them down is what moves the number next. Reproduce:
#   scripts/check_tcb_panic_freedom.sh --list.
#
# 2026-11-04 (later): 205 -> 202. The three sites the entry above named as
#   owed are paid, each by a TOTAL replacement, no counted panic swapped for
#   an uncounted one:
#     * trust-vcgen generate/inferred_return/calls.rs x1: the
#       `let Terminator::Call .. else { unreachable!() }` after `admits()`
#       (which itself destructures exactly that variant) now returns the
#       `Err` the function already returns two lines above for a
#       non-admitted call. Identical on every reachable path.
#     * trust-router full_verification/engine/mod.rs x2: `.expect` on the
#       primary and fallback engine `position()` lookups. `fallback_eligibility`
#       already adjudicates a `None` fallback as Terminal(NoRoute); the call
#       site now binds both indices by pattern and only enters the fallback
#       attempt on `(eligible, Some(index))`. A `None` primary — unreachable
#       for an item that arrived in a routed batch — skips the extra attempt
#       and keeps the primary evidence, which is fail-closed: a fallback is
#       only ever an additional attempt, never a requirement for a proof.
#   Validated: trust-router --lib full_verification 106/106, trust-vcgen
#   --lib inferred_return 27/27; counter 205 -> 202 is exactly these three
#   (expect 135 -> 133, unreachable 52 -> 51). Full lib suites at the land
#   head: trust-router 948 passed / 0 failed; trust-vcgen 5626 passed with
#   8 failures (six contracts, one len_witness_grounding, one
#   machine_faithful_contract_lane) that fail IDENTICALLY with this change's
#   one vcgen file swapped back to the base version — pre-existing, none in
#   inferred_return. Reproduce: scripts/check_tcb_panic_freedom.sh --list.
# 2026-11-12: 202 -> 145. No production Rust changed in this ratchet.
#   The previous population counted 157: it omitted the split compiler pass
#   under trust_verify/ (three production sites) and included fifteen sites
#   in descendants of cfg(test)-only files (five vcgen parity sites and ten
#   IR-bridge removed_bundle_members sites). Declarations now come from all
#   Rust files, including test-named parents, while only established test
#   ancestry excludes a counted candidate. Production imports, orphans and
#   ungrounded cycles remain counted. The corrected population is 145, not
#   evidence that 57 panics were removed. Reproduce on the unchanged Rust
#   sources of 401ce4326291 with the corrected counter:
#   bash scripts/check_tcb_panic_freedom.sh --print
#   The updater now refuses increases and malformed/overflowing baselines.
# 2026-11-17: 145 -> 137. Main (c57ca62cc1) measured 153, above this baseline
#   (OPEN_ISSUES TCB-PANIC-RATCHET-RED-1, now removed). The counter did not
#   change; sixteen production sites now refuse instead of panicking:
#   - trust-cg-bridge module_to_lir `lower_aggregate_inst`: six
#     `result0.expect("guarded")` become an `UnsupportedAggregate` refusal,
#     should a tracked-aggregate guard and its arm ever disagree;
#   - trust-cg-bridge object_replay/scalar_certificate.rs: the three
#     `try_into().unwrap()` in u16_at/u32_at/u64_at join the bounds-checked
#     Option chain, and the RET-word check compares the slice with the
#     encoded bytes, so a wrong-length slice is refused, not unwrapped;
#   - trust-wasm-bridge `exact_non_empty_scalar_target_binding`: six
#     "blockers empty only when ..." expects become one let-else that
#     returns a `non-empty-scalar-binding-component-missing` blocker.
#   153 - 16 = 137. Reproduce: bash scripts/check_tcb_panic_freedom.sh --list
137
